From 9a5abd49054ca45ed6700cf1d42f0f9092983589 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Sugiyama <yuichis@ricsec.co.jp>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:24:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] pta: veraison_attestation: keep the signing key in
 secure storage

The PTA could only sign with the test key embedded at build time,
which is why CFG_VERAISON_ATTESTATION_PTA_TEST_KEY had to be enabled
and why the README lists the lack of a trusted key as a known
limitation.

Without the test key, let the PTA own its signing key: an ECDSA P-256
key generated on first use and kept in the private secure storage of
the PTA, the way the attestation PTA keeps its RSA key. The key never
leaves the PTA. The calling TA only gets the public key, through the
new GET_PUBLIC_KEY command, so a compromised TA cannot sign evidence
of its own making with it. IMPORT_KEY installs a key generated and
registered elsewhere, for a provisioning flow; it is refused with the
test key.

A key, once stored, is never replaced: IMPORT_KEY only works before
the PTA has a key, and there is no command to generate another one.
Every TA's evidence is signed with the same key, so no TA may change
it under the others, and the store never has to replace an object in
place, which not every file system does atomically. Only user TAs may
open a session with the PTA, as with the system PTA, so a client in
the normal world cannot provision the key either.

With the CAAM ECC driver (CFG_NXP_CAAM_ECC_DRV=y) the generated key is
a CAAM black key: key.d holds its serialized form, a blob bound to the
device, which the driver unwraps inside the CAAM when signing, so the
plain private key never exists in memory.

The PSA instance-id was a constant unrelated to the key. Derive it from
the public key of the key in use, as RFC 9783 (section 4.2.1) allows
for the IAK identifier (0x01 followed by the SHA-256 of the SEC 1
uncompressed point), so the verifier can tie the evidence to the
registered key. The value reported for the test key therefore changes.

Upstream-Status: Submitted [https://github.com/OP-TEE/optee_os/pull/8109]
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Sugiyama <yuichis@ricsec.co.jp>
